 \begin{abstract}
-Direction normalization retains an observation's direction from a chosen origin and discards its distance. We establish when this compression imposes classification loss across all origins and how one radial bit repairs it. For absolutely continuous binary populations in dimension at least two, directional Bayes risk converges uniformly at distant origins to orthogonal-projection risk. This limit gives an all-origin loss criterion; planar signed-density cores provide explicit lower certificates. For half-annuli of radii $0.8$ and $1.2$ with horizontal separation $1$, a positive loss floor holds at each fixed displacement $0<h\leq\sqrt{3/5}$ and at the overlapping configuration $h=1$. At $h=0.5$, one radius bit about the midpoint selects between two angular rules and restores zero Bayes risk throughout a certified threshold interval. The same interval gives a sharp bounded-radius-error guarantee; one bit is the minimum radial addition to exact direction. Controlled neural studies comprise 500 final candidate fits and 250 validation-selected evaluations. In the separate repair experiment, direction-only error is $10.8724\%$; one-bit networks make three errors across 500,000 observations ($0.0006\%$). The results connect a global impossibility criterion, computable loss certificates and decision-preserving recovery.
+Direction normalization suppresses scale variation by retaining an observation's direction and discarding its distance from a chosen origin. When distance distinguishes classes, this compression can remove information that no classifier can recover. We investigate whether changing the origin prevents that loss and how little radial information restores the decision when it cannot. For absolutely continuous binary populations in dimension at least two, we prove that directional Bayes risk converges uniformly at distant origins to orthogonal-projection risk. This limit yields a criterion for unavoidable loss over every origin; planar signed-density regions provide computable lower certificates. A specified half-moon family exhibits positive global loss throughout a proved range of disjoint configurations and in an overlapping configuration. For one disjoint configuration, a shared near/far radius bit restores every class decision. Its exact threshold interval also gives a sharp radius-error tolerance. Controlled neural studies comprise 500 candidate fits and 250 selected evaluations. In the repair experiment, direction-only error is $10.8724\%$; one-bit networks make three errors across 500,000 observations ($0.0006\%$). The results connect geometric information loss, a minimal radial repair and its measurement accuracy.
 \end{abstract}
